    "result_text": "The build passes cleanly (exit 0, 47 tests, 0 failures/errors), including the new `Cp34Tests`.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `ageBand` derivation feature. The app derives it from an optional ISO `birthDate` in the create request, computed against the owner's `registrationDate`.\n\nChanges:\n\n1. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 Added a `birthDate` field (`@Column(name = \"birth_date\")`) with getter/setter, and a derived `getAgeBand()` method:\n   - `null` when `birthDate` or `registrationDate` is absent\n   - `MINOR` when under 18, `ADULT` when 18\u201364, `SENIOR` when 65+\n   - uses `Period.between(birthDate, registrationDate).getYears()`\n\n2. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 Added `birthDate` (`format: date`, optional) to `OwnerFields` (the write-side input) and a read-only `ageBand` enum (`MINOR`/`ADULT`/`SENIOR`) to the `Owner` schema. The DTOs are generated from this at build time, and the MapStruct `OwnerMapper` maps both fields automatically by name (input \u2192 entity, entity \u2192 response).\n\n3. **Schema files** (h2, hsqldb, mysql, postgres) \u2014 Added a `birth_date DATE` column to the `owners` table so JPA persistence works. Tests run on the hsqldb profile.\n\nThe controller flow already sets `registrationDate` (from the request or defaulting to today) before serialization, so `ageBand` is computed against the correct effective date. In the test, `birthDate=1990-01-01` against a default registration date of 2026-08-14 yields age 36 \u2192 `ADULT`, as asserted.",
